e space can at most stay the same, never decrease.\n\n## 2.1.3 Details on steps (F₂-St.2) and (F₂-St.3)\n\nImportant tools for (F₂-St.2) are the following theorems:\n\n**Theorem 38** (Böttcher–Schacht–Taraz [24, Theorem 2]). *For every* γ > 0 *and arbitrary* ρ ∈ Z_{≥2} *and* Δ ∈ Z_{≥2} *there exist numbers* β = β(γ, Δ) > 0 *and* n₀ = n₀(γ, Δ) *such that the following is true: for every graph* G *with* |G| ≥ n₀ *and* δ(G) ≥ ((ρ−1)/ρ + γ)|G|, *and for every graph* H *having* |G| = |H|, Δ(H) ≤ Δ *and* bw(H) ≤ β|H|, *and admitting a bandwidth-β|H|-labeling* b: V(H) → {1, . . . , |H|} *and a* (ρ + 1)-colouring* h: V(H) → {0, 1, . . . , ρ} *which is* (8ρβ|H|, 4ρβ|H|)-zero-free w.r.t. b *and has* |h^{-1}(0)| ≤ β|H|, *there is an embedding* H ↪ G.\n\n**Theorem 39** (Böttcher–Heinig–Taraz [22, Theorem 3]). *For every* γ > 0 *and every* Δ ∈ Z *there exist numbers* β = β(γ, Δ) > 0 *and* n₀ = n₀(γ, Δ) ∈ Z *such that the following is true: for every balanced bipartite graph* G *with* |G| ≥ n₀ *and* δ(G) ≥ (1/4 + γ)|G|, *and for every balanced bipartite graph* H *with* |H| = |G|, Δ(H) ≤ Δ *and* bw(H) ≤ β|H|, *there is an embedding* H ↪ G.\n\nThe lower bound of terrestrial magnitude provided by (I4) depends on a recent theorem of Châu, DeBiasio and Kierstead (who say [35, p. 17, Section 5, I.5] that by optimising their proof one may not push the bound further down than to about n₀ = 10^5, but who do express optimism as to the possibility of getting rid of the lower bound on |·| altogether):\n\n**Theorem 40** (Komlós–Sárközy–Szemerédi [102, Theorem 1], Jamshed [90, Chapter 3]; explicit lower bound on |G| proved by Châu–DeBiasio–Kierstead [35, Theorem 7]). *For every graph* G *with* |G| ≥ 2 · 10^8 *and* δ(G) ≥ 2/3 |G| *there exists an embedding* C^2_{|G|} ↪ G.\n\n## 2.1.3.1 Auxiliary statements for step (F₂-St.3)\n\nThere is a simple algebraic lemma underlying Lemma 43, and for this lemma it appears that free modules over principal ideal domains provide the natural generality. With a view towards Section 2.2 which studies H̃(G) vis-à-vis the Z-module Z_1(G; Z), let us opt for this generality right-away, at negligible additional cost, but with more insight into the underlying mechanism. If R is a commutative ring, M a free R-module and B ⊆ M an R-basis of M, then for every v ∈ M we write (λ_{B,v,b})_{b∈B} ∈ R^B the unique element of R^B (cofinitely-many components zero) with v = ∑_{b∈B} λ_{B,v,b} b. Then for every b ∈ B the map λ_{B,·,b}: v ↦ λ_{B,v,b} is an element of Hom_R(M, R) (which elsewhere is often denoted by b*). Moreover, we define Supp_B(v) := {b ∈ B : λ_{B,v,b} ≠ 0} ⊆ B. We can now formulate a slight generalization of [113, Lemma 1] and [7, Corollary 3.2], which is the algebraic mechanism underlying Lemma 43:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOperations Research Letters 44 (2016) 191–195 (https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/25202/1-s2.0-S016763771600002X-main.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Olver / Operations Research Letters 44 (2016) 191–195 ... This was resolved by Goyal et al. [8], who proved ... for their help in the exposition of the main proof. ... Shepherd, The VPN conjecture is true, J.\nN. Olver / Operations Research Letters 44 (2016) 191–195\n195\n3. Connection to the VPN conjecture\nresolution to the Generalized VPN Conjecture would imply that\nthe RND problem for U(T b) can be solved optimally in polynomial\ntime.\nA well-known conjecture in the area was the VPN Conjecture.\nThis was resolved by Goyal et al. [8], who proved\nAcknowledgments\nTheorem 10 ([8]). There is an optimal solution to the RND problem\nwith oblivious routing for the hose model whose support is a tree.\nThe author is very grateful to Alexandre Fréchette and Bruce\nShepherd, both for asking the question that led to this note, and\nfor their help in the exposition of the main proof. The author also\nthanks an anonymous referee for a careful reading. This research\nwas supported by a V