z [1], we also consider (𝑘+ 1)-coloured graphs where one colour\nis used only sparingly, as follows. A 𝛽-block of {1, … , 𝑛} is an interval of the type {(𝑖−1)⌈𝛽𝑛⌉+\n1, … , 𝑖⌈𝛽𝑛⌉} for some 1 ⩽𝑖⩽𝛽−1. A (𝑘+ 1)-colouring 𝜒∶{1, … , 𝑛} →{0, 1, … , 𝑘} is said to be\n(𝑧, 𝛽)-zero-free if, among every 𝑧consecutive 𝛽-blocks at most one of them uses colour 0.\nDefinition 2.2 ((𝑧, 𝛽, Δ, 𝑘)-Hamiltonian). A graph 𝐺on 𝑛vertices is (𝑧, 𝛽, Δ, 𝑘)-Hamiltonian if\n𝐺contains all graphs 𝐻on 𝑛vertices with Δ(𝐻) ⩽Δ which admit an ordering that certifies\nsimultaneously a bandwidth of at most 𝛽𝑛and a (𝑧, 𝛽)-zero-free (𝑘+ 1)-colouring.\nWe remark that the embedded guest graphs in this definition cover (𝑘−1)st powers of cycles 𝐻\n(of any order), when 𝛽> 0, 𝑧⩽1∕𝛽and 𝑛is large enough. Moreover, a (𝛽, Δ, 𝑘+ 1)-Hamiltonian\ngraph is (𝑧, 𝛽, Δ, 𝑘)-Hamiltonian for any 𝑘, Δ, 𝑧, 𝛽. Given this terminology, we can now formally\nstate the aforementioned result of Böttcher, Schacht and Taraz [8].\nTheorem 2.3 (Bandwidth Theorem). For any 𝜇> 0, 𝑘⩾2, Δ > 0, there exists 𝑧, 𝛽> 0 such that\nevery sufficiently large graph 𝐺on 𝑛vertices with 𝛿(𝐺) ⩾𝑘−1\n𝑘𝑛+ 𝜇𝑛is (𝑧, 𝛽, Δ, 𝑘)-Hamiltonian.\n 1460244x, 2023, 3, Downloaded from https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/plms.12552 by Spanish Cochrane National Provision (Ministerio de Sanidad), Wiley Online Library on [24/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdoes #961 - working report - Erdős Problem a Day (https://www.erdosproblemaday.com/report/961)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Since \\(x>k\\geq m\\), \\(N\\geq2m\\). ...   * The conjectured bound in problem 683 with \\(H(m)=m^{1+c}\\) would give\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan